UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Saint Lea (died c. 383) is honored as a fourth-century saint of the Roman Catholic Church on the authority of Jerome. Everything known of Lea of Rome comes from the testimony of her dear friend, the learned Saint Jerome. He was a scholarly monk, best known for translating the Bible into Latin (the Vulgate), and he is the Church''s only source on her; her biographical details are otherwise unknown.

Lea was a Roman noblewoman, born to wealth and privilege, and a contemporary of Jerome. Soon after she married she was widowed, leaving her very well provided for financially. Rather than live as a rich widow, she entered a community of consecrated virgins in the city and gave up all her money and social standing. In her later years she was named prioress of the convent. She also served the community led by Marcella, doing the work of a menial servant, and afterward became the group''s superior.

She appears to have died in 384, while Saint Jerome and Saint Marcella were busy reading and studying Psalm 73. Jerome wrote to Saint Marcella to pass the news of her death on to others in the city of Rome. His letter said that Lea had died, describing her as austere and obedient and as a woman of remarkable penances. He called her "blessed," stressing that her virtues made her worthy of heaven. Jerome gives no biography of Lea, since he assumes that Marcella knows her, and he dwells instead on her virtues.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Lea", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx07crg01mku7z5mjt7vf0e'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-lea'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = '45f2f3f7b0e24741bf232b1117077434';

