UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Edmund the Martyr, who died on 20 November 869, is also called St Edmund or Edmund of East Anglia. From about 855 he ruled East Anglia as king, and held the throne to the end of his life. Because the Vikings ravaged East Anglia and destroyed any evidence from his own time, few historical facts about him are known. His coins and those of Æthelweard of East Anglia used the same moneyers, which indicates that Edmund succeeded Æthelweard. He is thought to have been East Anglian by origin, yet writers of the 12th century produced fictitious accounts of his family, his succession and his rule as king. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records his death, stating that he was killed in 869 once the Great Heathen Army had pushed into East Anglia. Medieval versions of his life and martyrdom disagree: either he died in battle against the Great Heathen Army, or he was captured and killed when he would not meet the demand of the Viking leaders that he renounce Christ. Following his death, a popular cult arose, and the Church canonised him. From around the time Wessex absorbed East Anglia in 918, a series of coins was minted in his memory, and about 986 Abbo of Fleury, a French monk, wrote an account of his life and martyrdom. In the 10th century his remains were translated from an unidentified place in East Anglia to Beodericsworth (modern Bury St Edmunds); in 1010 they were temporarily taken to London for safekeeping.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_the_Martyr", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx0epsl021cu7z53ovuoslr'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-edmund-the-martyr'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = '3647d5d405f92ee0c3101f7ef52947b2';

