UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Saint Balderic (also called Baudry) founded Montfaucon and was its first abbot. During the 7th century, France was home to Balderic, and to Beuve (or Bove or Bova), his sister. They were reputed, on the strength of oral tradition that Flodoard, Canon of Reims, recorded three centuries later, to have been the offspring of Sigebert I, king of Austrasia. It is also said that they were related to a King Dagobert (presumably Dagobert I of Austrasia). Balderic became a priest and later established, in the province of Lorraine, the monastery of Montfaucon. In 639 he founded, for Beuve, a convent at Reims, "St-Pierre-les-Dames". As abbess there she was followed by Dode (or Doda), the niece of both Beuve and Balderic. Balderic guided and taught Saint Wandregisel (also Wandrille), who lived at Montfaucon after leaving his wife in 628. The abbot died at Reims, where he had gone to see his sister; 16 October is his feast day.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balderic_of_Montfaucon", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx0kiew02fcu7z5x6zlppaw'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-balderic-abbot-of-montfaucon'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = '0191192369b5e3c2249d380dcc695a8c';

UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Berthold, an oblate and Benedictine lay brother, died in 1111. His Anglo-saxon parents ultimately fled to Parma, Italy, after the Norman conquest of England, and he was born there. He was putatively promised to the monastery when he was 12 years old. Most of his life was spent serving nuns at the Benedictine Monastery of Sant''Alessandro in Parma. In his younger years he went on various pilgrimages, one to Rome and another to Isere, where Saint Anthony of Vienne is buried. After returning to Parma, he gave help in the monastery and to the infirm in its hospital. He was never canonized, although a number of miracles were credited to prayers addressed to him or meetings with him.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berthold_of_Parma", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx0kkz302ffu7z5w7m66u0j'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-berthold-of-parma'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = 'd91ec7d3a693b7146e1f43716bd9c77d';

UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Mortlach had three known bishops, and Beóán was the first. In non-Gaelic contexts his name may also appear as Beanus, Beoanus and Beyn, and it means "lively one". Walter Bower, who follows John of Fordun, writes that King Máel Coluim II of Scotland founded the bishopric to thank God for his victories over the Scandinavians, doing so in his seventh regnal year. Bower names "the first bishop" as "Beyn, a saintly man," calls him "worthy of the episcopal office," and says he was "elevated to this see" through "the Lord Pope Benedict VIII" on a request from the king. According to the Aberdeen Registrum, King Máel Coluim issued a charter at Forfar to Bishop Beóán, giving him the churches and lands of Clova along with Dulmech, which has not been identified. The Aberdeen Breviary listed "Bishop Beóán" as a saint with a commemoration on 26 October. There was also another Beóán, perhaps the one who appears in St. Cathróe of Metz''s Life, who was commemorated on 16 December. The two were frequently confused.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Be%C3%B3%C3%A1n_of_Mortlach", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx0klov02fiu7z5smtpo4mq'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-beoan-of-mortlach'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = '4b53d834a8b99ff754f845ecccd22d12';

UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'Buonfiglio dei Monaldi, among the founders of the Servite Order, is also sometimes called "Saint Bonfilius". Bonfilius lived c. 1040 – c. 1115 and was an Italian monk and bishop who is counted a saint. Born in Osimo, near Ancona, he joined, as a monk, the Benedictine house Santa Maria di Storaco, near Filottrano. Although he was the youngest member there, he was regarded as the most learned in scripture of the congregation, and the monks chose him as abbot of the monastery. He was elected bishop of Foligno at some point between 1072 and 1078. In 1078 Bonfilius donated to the canons of the Folignano church, and in 1080 he was encouraging Mainardo in founding the abbey of Sassovivo. In 1096 he realized his wish to travel to the Holy Land, joining the forces of the First Crusade as a pilgrim. As he travelled there, the companions with whom he had set out died one after another, and he arrived in Jerusalem alone. Until 1104 he lived there as a hermit; then he went back to Italy. By then a certain Andreas had filled the vacant see of Foligno, so he went back to the Storaco abbey, and later spent his final years once more living as a hermit, near Cingoli at St Maria of la Far. There Bonfilius died of natural causes on 27 September 1115. The Sylvestrines'' founder, St. Sylvester Gozzolini, was his first biographer and drew inspiration from Bonfilius'' life.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonfilius", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
    "contentChecksum" = NULL,
    "updatedAt" = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE content."id" = 'cmvx0kohr02flu7z5bdvtfq7z'
  AND content."slug" = 'saint-bonfilius-of-foligno'
  AND md5(content."payload"->>'biography') = 'e732fb0cae47e3064e952bb68c33bfaa';

UPDATE "PublishedContent" AS content
SET "payload" = content."payload" || jsonb_build_object('biography', 'A virgin martyr from Asia Minor, St. Charitina of Amisus was noted for strict chastity and piety. She passed her days in fasting, prayer and study. Under Emperor Diocletian, her example led many to convert to Christianity, and she was arrested in Amisus, a city in Pontus. She was tortured and put to death, and her body was cast into the sea in 304.'::text) || jsonb_build_object('provenance', '{"article": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charitina_of_Amisus", "biography": "rewritten"}'::jsonb),
